WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.471
Welcome to Module One: System and Server Bring-up.

2
00:00:03.801 --> 00:00:07.933
It's how a pallet of GPU servers becomes a working AI factory.

3
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:13.099
Picture a team receiving thirty-two DGX nodes on a loading dock.

4
00:00:13.429 --> 00:00:17.496
The deadline is Friday, so they rack everything and power it all on at once.

5
00:00:18.296 --> 00:00:21.681
First problem: the nodes arrived on mixed firmware versions.

6
00:00:22.011 --> 00:00:25.289
Half the GPUs throw errors that nobody can reproduce.

7
00:00:26.089 --> 00:00:29.324
Second problem: one swapped cable in the compute fabric.

8
00:00:29.654 --> 00:00:33.167
A training job crawls, and it takes days to find the wrong port.

9
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:37.543
Third problem: one BMC still has its default password.

10
00:00:37.873 --> 00:00:41.066
Anyone on the management network could power-cycle that node.

11
00:00:41.866 --> 00:00:44.419
So the team starts again with a disciplined sequence.

12
00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:47.257
Plan, rack, power, cable, update firmware,

13
00:00:47.307 --> 00:00:50.181
secure, then validate, for every node, in order.

14
00:00:50.981 --> 00:00:54.089
Lesson one walks through that bring-up sequence step by step.

15
00:00:54.419 --> 00:00:56.118
You'll learn why order matters:

16
00:00:56.168 --> 00:00:59.446
firmware before validation, and security before production.

17
00:01:00.246 --> 00:01:02.649
It also maps the networks of an AI factory.

18
00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:07.687
Compute, storage, in-band, and out-of-band management each get their own fabric.

19
00:01:08.487 --> 00:01:10.210
Lesson two covers the BMC,

20
00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:14.303
a small computer that manages the server even when it's off.

21
00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:19.170
You'll lock down out-of-band access and see what the TPM adds to a trusted boot.

22
00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:24.336
Lesson three brings every HGX system to one firmware baseline.

23
00:01:24.666 --> 00:01:27.539
You'll also learn to spot faults in logs and health tools.

24
00:01:28.339 --> 00:01:32.534
Lesson four plans power and cooling before a single server is installed.

25
00:01:32.864 --> 00:01:38.062
One DGX can draw over ten kilowatts, so racks, circuits, and airflow all matter.

26
00:01:38.862 --> 00:01:41.308
Lesson five is cables and transceivers.

27
00:01:41.638 --> 00:01:43.803
You'll tell copper from optical,

28
00:01:43.853 --> 00:01:47.540
and match OSFP and QSFP modules to the right distance.

29
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:51.917
Lesson six validates the hardware before anyone trains on it.

30
00:01:52.247 --> 00:01:57.423
It checks GPUs, NVLink, and the network, then configures third-party storage.

31
00:01:58.223 --> 00:02:02.824
Back on the loading dock, all thirty-two nodes pass validation on the first try.

32
00:02:03.154 --> 00:02:05.792
Every node shares one firmware baseline,

33
00:02:05.842 --> 00:02:08.949
every cable is mapped, and every BMC is locked.

34
00:02:09.749 --> 00:02:12.665
That discipline is exactly what the exam rewards.

35
00:02:12.995 --> 00:02:16.286
Questions describe a real bring-up and ask what went wrong,

36
00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:17.702
or which step comes next.

37
00:02:18.502 --> 00:02:22.271
This domain is thirty-one percent of the exam, one of the two biggest.

38
00:02:22.601 --> 00:02:24.876
Lesson one starts with the bring-up sequence.
